1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:10,300
This is the millwright, the lab's infrastructure provisioner. It onboards members, scaffolds projects and health-checks the environment, one lab at a time.

2
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:22,399
Its file says what it does: onboard members onto machines, scaffold new projects with their repo, channel and data folders, keep the machines registry, and health-check an environment.

3
00:00:22,399 --> 00:00:37,700
Every write starts as a dry run that shows the full list of commands and waits for the PI. And it never pushes to main: it opens a branch and a pull request.

4
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:48,600
It never generates, stores or transmits a private key; only public keys leave your machine. It never writes into immutable data and never deletes data.

5
00:00:48,600 --> 00:01:03,500
It never touches the mothballed folder and never tidies the lab folder on its own: it reports, and the PI decides. And it does not act on shared infrastructure without PI sign-off.

6
00:01:03,899 --> 00:01:10,400
A real run, read-only. The member asks for a health check of their murmurent install.

7
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:25,299
The verdict is Concerns: the install runs, but the shared agents are not linked and the hooks use another Python. Each problem comes with the command that fixes it, and nothing was changed.

8
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:40,200
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the millwright to scaffold a project, onboard a member, or check the install. It shows the plan first and asks the PI to confirm.

9
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:56,000
The first line is the verdict: Provisioned, Skipped or Failed. Then what it did, and what it left for you to run on your own machine, such as adding your own key.
